{"name":"Jugendstil Green","hex":"#c3c8b3","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"jugendstil","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The German equivalent of art nouveau."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/jugendstil"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Jugendstil","blocks":[{"text":"Jugendstil was an artistic movement, particularly in the decorative arts, that was influential primarily in Germany, Austria, and elsewhere in Europe to a lesser extent from about 1895 until about 1910. It was the German and Austrian counterpart of Art Nouveau. The members of the movement were reacting against the historicism and neo-classicism of the official art and architecture academies. It took its name from the art journal Jugend, founded by the German artist Georg Hirth. It was especially active in the graphic arts and interior decoration."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Mathildenhoehe-ernst-ludwig-haus-076.jpg/330px-Mathildenhoehe-ernst-ludwig-haus-076.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jugendstil","wikidata":"Q1294605"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Otto_Eckmann_-_Jugend_Nr._14%2C_1896.jpg/500px-Otto_Eckmann_-_Jugend_Nr._14%2C_1896.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Otto_Eckmann_-_Jugend_Nr._14%2C_1896.jpg/500px-Otto_Eckmann_-_Jugend_Nr._14%2C_1896.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOtto_Eckmann_-_Jugend_Nr._14%2C_1896.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/La_maison_de_Peter_Behrens_%28Mus%C3%A9e_de_la_colonie_d%27artistes%2C_Darmstadt%29_%288728647639%29.jpg/500px-La_maison_de_Peter_Behrens_%28Mus%C3%A9e_de_la_colonie_d%27artistes%2C_Darmstadt%29_%288728647639%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/La_maison_de_Peter_Behrens_%28Mus%C3%A9e_de_la_colonie_d%27artistes%2C_Darmstadt%29_%288728647639%29.jpg/500px-La_maison_de_Peter_Behrens_%28Mus%C3%A9e_de_la_colonie_d%27artistes%2C_Darmstadt%29_%288728647639%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedi